{"tool_name":"xflow","contents":["oeb","bioschemas","biotools"],"fetched_metadata":{"biotools":{"id":"XFlow","home":"https://github.com/optimusmoose/jsms","summary":"An algorithm for extracting ion chromatograms.","addition_date":"2020-01-14T19:36:13Z","last_update_date":"2024-11-24T20:38:53.969457Z","biotools_curie":"biotools:XFlow","edit_permission":{"type":"public"},"function":[{"operation":[{"term":"Chromatogram visualisation","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_3203"},{"term":"MRM/SRM","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_3636"}]}],"name":"XFlow","owner":"Pub2Tools","publication":[{"doi":"10.1101/2019.12.27.889253"},{"doi":"10.1371/journal.pone.0227659","metadata":{"abstract":"Mass spectrometry is a fundamental tool for modern proteomics. The increasing availability of mass spectrometry data paired with the increasing sensitivity and fidelity of the instruments necessitates new and more potent analytical methods. To that end, we have created and present XFlow, a feature detection algorithm for extracting ion chromatograms from MS1 LC-MS data. XFlow is a parameter-free procedurally agnostic feature detection algorithm that utilizes the latent properties of ion chromatograms to resolve them from the surrounding noise present in MS1 data. XFlow is designed to function on either profile or centroided data across different resolutions and instruments. This broad applicability lends XFlow strong utility as a one-size-fits-all method for MS1 analysis or target acquisition for MS2. XFlow is written in Java and packaged with JS-MS, an open-source mass spectrometry analysis toolkit.","authors":[{"name":"Gutierrez M."},{"name":"Smith R."}],"citationCount":1,"date":"2020-10-01T00:00:00Z","journal":"PLoS ONE","title":"XFlow: An algorithm for extracting ion chromatograms"},"pmcid":"PMC7580946","pmid":"33091000"}],"topic":[{"term":"Proteomics","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_0121"},{"term":"Proteomics experiment","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_3520"},{"ter